    "question": {
      ":question/id": "da912c13-7c9a-5435-9b52-c4731731089d",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{3x-3}+\\frac{1}{2-3x})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{3x-3}+\\frac{1}{2-3x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-3|-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-3x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{3x-3}{2-3x}|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3x-3}{2-3x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
